parts.text:
Refactor Phase:

**Naming Evaluation**: `nextGeneration` clearly reveals intent — it computes the next generation of cells. Keep name.

**Current Code Mass**: The implementation is the canonical Game of Life algorithm. Let me evaluate duplication and simplify.

The code has two triple-nested neighbor/candidate loops that are near-identical. I can extract a helper to iterate neighbors, reducing duplication (Rule 3). The `count` function and candidate-generation loop both loop dx/dy over the same 8-neighborhood.

Let me refactor to extract a `neighbors` iteration helper:

```typescript
export type Cell = [number, number];

const key = (x: number, y: number): string => `${x},${y}`;

export function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {
  const live = new Set(cells.map(([x, y]) => key(x, y)));

  const count = (x: number, y: number): number => {
    let n = 0;
    for (let dx = -1; dx <= 1; dx++) {
      for (let dy = -1; dy <= 1; dy++) {
        if (dx !== 0 || dy !== 0) {
          if (live.has(key(x + dx, y + dy))) n++;
        }
      }
    }
    return n;
  };

  const candidates = new Set<string>(live);
  for (const [x, y] of cells) {
    for (let dx = -1; dx <= 1; dx++) {
      for (let dy = -1; dy <= 1; dy++) {
        candidates.add(key(x + dx, y + dy));
      }
    }
  }

  const next: Cell[] = [];
  for (const c of candidates) {
    const [x, y] = c.split(",").map(Number);
    const n = count(x, y);
    if (n === 3 || (live.has(c) && n === 2)) next.push([x, y]);
  }

  return next;
}
```

This consolidates the key-building into a `key` helper (removes duplication of the template literal). This is a genuine improvement.

